  "name": "CATECHOLAMINES PLASMA",
  "code": "1053",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"120\" data-end=\"374\">The <strong data-start=\"124\" data-end=\"149\">Plasma Catecholamines</strong> test measures the levels of <strong data-start=\"178\" data-end=\"206\">epinephrine (adrenaline)</strong>, <strong data-start=\"208\" data-end=\"242\">norepinephrine (noradrenaline)</strong>, and sometimes <strong data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"270\">dopamine</strong> in the blood. These are stress-related hormones released by the adrenal medulla and sympathetic nerves.</p>\n<p data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"407\">This test is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"409\" data-end=\"705\">\n<li data-start=\"409\" data-end=\"505\">\n<p data-start=\"411\" data-end=\"505\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"420\" data-end=\"454\">catecholamine-secreting tumors</strong> such as <strong data-start=\"463\" data-end=\"483\">pheochromocytoma</strong> and <strong data-start=\"488\" data-end=\"505\">paraganglioma</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"506\" data-end=\"639\">\n<p data-start=\"508\" data-end=\"589\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"517\" data-end=\"555\">episodic or sustained hypertension</strong>, especially when associated with:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"639\">\n<li data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"605\">\n<p data-start=\"594\" data-end=\"605\">Headaches</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"608\" data-end=\"620\">\n<p data-start=\"610\" data-end=\"620\">Sweating</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"623\" data-end=\"639\">\n<p data-start=\"625\" data-end=\"639\">Palpitations</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"640\" data-end=\"705\">\n<p data-start=\"642\" data-end=\"705\">Monitor response to treatment or recurrence after tumor removal</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition / Cause</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pheochromocytoma / Paraganglioma</strong></td>\n<td>High levels of norepinephrine and/or epinephrine strongly suggest these tumors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neuroblastoma (in children)</strong></td>\n<td>May present with elevated plasma catecholamines, especially dopamine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severe stress or trauma</strong></td>\n<td>Acute stress, pain, or trauma can transiently raise catecholamines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Heart failure or myocardial infarction</strong></td>\n<td>Sympathetic activation may increase levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic sympathetic overactivity can cause elevated plasma catecholamines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Withdrawal or rebound from medications</strong></td>\n<td>E.g., stopping clonidine or beta-blockers abruptly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></td>\n<td>Triggers a sympathetic response, increasing catecholamine release</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
